    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5802",
      "problem": "Graph the curve corresponding to the equation $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x-2)(x+1)^2.$",
      "worked_solution": "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-2)(0+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238854/assets/1b6886faa1ecd42e9539b9f6867ff0f629f6ae99366d8b9f397f82c5212ebffd.png)",
